blocked and that's what will  then feed through into a faster response  into emergency departments and a faster  response for ambulance so when are  people going to see that because it's  not just that money I mean that money is  very welcome of course everyone will say  that but this is years and years of  under investment in the health services  no well I don't agree with that we put  of extra money into the Health Service  every single year and there's record  levels of investment into the Health  Service in real terms we've got uh  thousands more doctors and thousands  more nurses working in the Health  Service you know there are 130 000  vacancies in the Health Service there  are huge discrepancies in those figures  well no look there are significantly  more doctors and nurses working in the  Health Service we do think it's  important to look at Workforce uh in the  long term that's why the chance to set  that out in the Autumn statement and  that's why the Health Service is working  on that long-term Workforce for a plan  at the moment which will be published in  the coming months okay let's come on to  your brief and the transport strikes  there was reporting before Christmas  that ministers have got involved at the  last minute and have put a block on a  deal being signed between the union and  the rail delivery groups is that true no  that absolutely isn't true in fact since  I became transport secretary a couple of  months ago I met all the union leaders I  tried to change the tone of the  discussions and I said that ministers  would help facilitate the trade unions  and the employers that's the train  operating company's Network rail getting  around the table I made sure there was a  new and improved offer that went to  trade Union members that's why two of  the unions have settled on network rail  the rmt recommended though sadly that  their members should reject that but  even so a third of their members wanted  to accept it so look I think in the New  Year it's time for the rmt to get off  the picket line background the  negotiating table the ministers will do  what we can to facilitate them coming to  a deal with the employers I want to get  these rail disputes sorted out for the  benefit of the traveling public so  there's no Clause that's been asserted  at the last minute about driver-only  trainers driver only change is not a new  thing they've been um they've been in  place the unions it's always been a red  line they've been in place since the  1990s 55 of people that use trains are  all in Services where there are driver  only operation Workforce reform is  important we've got to get rid of some  of these outdated working practices so  for example we can have a proper  seven-day Railway where the rail service  isn't dependent on people being rosted  to work on their days off that doesn't  deliver a good service for for  passengers so we need modern Railway a  there's a fair and reasonable offer on  the table and I hope the rmt looked to  take it I mean you know that the rmts  say that they work seven days a week and  it's not an issue about rostering they  say that there's not enough staff as  well which is perhaps part of the  problem on those Sundays when people  aren't no look this is this is because  there are days of the week when people  don't have to work they're not  contracted to work and the rail service  depends on what's called rest day  working which is where people are  rostered to work on their days off and  if they don't want to come to work the  training companies find it very  difficult to run those Services that's  not a way to run a modern Railway we  need a proper seven-day Railway where  people are rostered on each day and have  to come to work on each day they're  contracted to work so that you can  deliver better services for passengers  it's that sort of Reform with a fair pay  package that we want to see to resolve  this decision has that been offered  there has been a fair and reasonable  offer on the table which is comparable  to what most people listening to this  program will have got across the economy  two trade unions accepted it that offer  from Network rail the rmt are holding  out against it and I hope they get  background in negotiating table off the  picket lines and we can try and hammer  out a deal between the employers and the  trading they're saying they want to talk  to you they said they've had no contact  for the past couple of weeks well no  look they met the rail minister so the  employers trade unions and the rail  Minister met before Christmas the rail  ministers continue to talk to them  across the Christmas period and we're  looking to have more meetings set up  next week I'd much rather those meetings  took place this week instead of strikes  taking place  um and that would be my message to the  Trade union why are they taking place  this week then well because the Trade  union  because the trade unions decided they  wanted to go on strike this week which  is deeply unhelpful damages the rail  industry damages the interest of the  people that work in it I want to see  them back around the table we can try  and hammer out a deal between the  employers and the trade Unions that's  what I want to see happen that's what  I'm doing working really hard with the  rail minister to try and sort out is  there going to be an increase in the  offer to these striking workers in terms  of their benefits well look there's a  fair and reasonable payoff for on the  table people that there's not a  bottomless pit of taxpayers money here  the taxpayers put a huge amount of  investment into the rail industry over  the last few years when it was hit with  a huge impact from the pandemic when  people weren't traveling and I think  it's you've got to have an offer that's  both fair to the people that work in the  industry but it's also fair to the  taxpayer that's picking up the tab and  that's the balance that we're trying to  strike I mean just a broader thought  about the government that you you  represent in Europe seeing all this  strike actions how does that feel  working in a government that has so many  people within the public sector and the  private sector out on strike because of  the the say the economy and the way that  they feel the country is being right  well look it's not a surprise at the  moment with inflation being very high  we're seeing High inflation you know  driven by uh the war in Ukraine the high  energy prices it's not surprising that  people are under pressure that's why the  governments try to make reasonable  payoffers in the public sector it's why  for example we've accepted the  independent pay review borders in the  health service for example which was  above the government's own affordable  ability constraints but accepted that  offer in full to try and give people a  fair pay rise but look I accept things  are difficult at the moment we want to  try and get inflation back under control  that's the way that will help everybody  in the economy that's definitely the  ambition and do you ex do you accept  that more can be done by government to  try and get this resolve people are  asking questions about where the Prime  Minister has been people are asking  questions about Parliament being  recalled this week to try and resolve  some of these issues well the Prime  Minister has been working I think had a  short break and for Christmas day it's  been working across the Christmas period  we put in place those uh offers before  Christmas or pay we've accepted the pay  review bodies recommendations in the  health service for example and I think  ministers are going to continue trying  to do what we can to resolve these  disputes but as I said it has to be fair  both for the people that work in the  public sector but also for the taxpayer  that has to meet those bills that's what  the government's going to continue  trying to do now you said you've not  been able to speak to the rmt so far  this week you'd like to but I'm going to  be speaking to little while's time what  would you want to say to to Mick Lynch  well I I had a perfectly constructive  meeting when I met Mick Lynch after I  took on this job I'd say look the real  Minister's trying to get some more  meetings sorted out history let's get  off the picket line let's get around the  negotiating table I made sure there was  an improved offer that went to Trade  union members let's try and Hammer out  the detail you get these disputes  resolved around the negotiating table  not on the picket line and that would be  my message to Mr Lynch okay Mark Harper  Transportation pleasure thank you very  much

